 "answer": {
  "candidates": [
   {
    "id": "C1",
    "quote": "In Python, variables are simply names that refer to objects. Variables do not need to be declared before they are assigned and they can even change type in the middle of a program.",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:dynamic-typing-names-refer-to-objects",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:dynamic-typing-names-refer-to-objects",
     "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:dynamic-typing-is-irreversible",
     "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:operator-overloading-blocks-type-deduction",
     "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:python-drops-compile-time-checking",
     "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:python-is-dynamically-typed",
     "written:2009-03-10_the-problem-with-integer-division:no-type-declarations-no-coercion"
    ],
    "statement": "Python is dynamically typed: the compiler ignores declarations and all type checking happens at run time, a deliberate corner-cutting simplification that stays safe because every type error is still caught before the interpreter can malfunction.",
    "time_local": "2009-01-13 09:17:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C2",
    "quote": "Python simply drops the compile-time type checking completely. This is entirely in line with Python’s “corner-cutting” philosophy as it’s a simplification of the implementation that does not affect the eventual safety, since all type errors are caught at run time",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:python-drops-compile-time-checking",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-02-10_python-s-use-of-dynamic-typing:python-drops-compile-time-checking",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:any-statements-in-class-body",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:callable-class-no-new-operator",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:class-dictionary-holds-methods",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:class-variables-fall-out",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:explicit-self-removes-special-cases",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:instance-as-classref-plus-dict",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:no-conformance-checking",
     "written:2010-06-21_new-style-classes:classes-as-afterthought-cut-corners"
    ],
    "statement": "Prefer the simplest possible implementation and let features fall out of it rather than be specially designed in, cutting corners wherever the gap to a fuller design is small.",
    "time_local": "2009-02-10 11:27:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C3",
    "quote": "In Python, all objects that can be named are said to be \"first class.\"",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:everything-first-class",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:everything-first-class",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:dynamic-modifiability-of-classes-and-objects",
     "written:2009-02-18_adding-support-for-user-defined-classes:type-structure-as-runtime-object",
     "written:2009-02-27_first-class-everything:common-c-data-structure",
     "written:2009-02-27_first-class-everything:first-class-everything",
     "written:2009-02-27_first-class-everything:methods-as-first-class-problem",
     "written:2009-04-24_metaclasses-and-extension-classes-a-k-a-:classes-as-first-class-objects"
    ],
    "statement": "Everything nameable in Python (functions, classes, methods, modules, types, even the type structure itself) is a first-class, introspectable object that can be passed around, stored, and inspected at run time.",
    "time_local": "2009-01-13 09:17:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C4",
    "quote": "For error handling, Python uses (non-resumable) exceptions.",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:non-resumable-exceptions",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:non-resumable-exceptions",
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:no-undefined-values",
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:overflow-raises-exception",
     "written:2009-03-06_how-exceptions-came-to-be-classes:exceptions-for-error-handling",
     "written:2010-06-23_method-resolution-order:rejection-matches-zen-errors-not-silent"
    ],
    "statement": "Errors are handled through exceptions and no operation ever returns a silently undefined value: when no correct result can be computed, an exception is raised rather than letting a bad value pass.",
    "time_local": "2009-01-13 09:17:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C5",
    "quote": "instead of using braces for statement grouping, it uses indentation",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:indentation-for-grouping",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-01-13_introduction-and-overview:indentation-for-grouping",
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:colon-before-indented-block",
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:indentation-from-abc",
     "written:2010-06-29_from-list-comprehensions-to-generator-ex:list-comprehensions-from-set-notation",
     "written:2011-07-08_karin-dewar-indentation-and-the-colon:indentation-to-make-text-neat-and-suggestive",
     "written:2013-10-24_why-python-uses-0-based-indexing:adjacency-invariant",
     "written:2013-10-24_why-python-uses-0-based-indexing:avoid-plus-minus-one-compensations"
    ],
    "statement": "Syntax should be clean and human-readable, using indentation (with a clarifying colon) for grouping instead of braces, so program text looks neat and suggests its meaning.",
    "time_local": "2009-01-13 09:17:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C6",
    "quote": "This is one area where Python violated the ABC-inspired philosophy of not requiring users to care about a uninteresting implementation details.",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:explicit-long-suffix-violates-philosophy",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:explicit-long-suffix-violates-philosophy",
     "written:2009-02-03_early-language-design-and-development:sequence-generalization",
     "written:2009-03-03_how-everything-became-an-executable-stat:everything-is-an-executable-statement",
     "written:2009-03-03_how-everything-became-an-executable-stat:uniform-statements-simplify-grammar-and-compiler",
     "written:2010-08-24_why-python-s-integer-division-floors:div-and-mod-go-together",
     "written:2013-10-24_why-python-uses-0-based-indexing:zero-based-half-open-defaults-elegant"
    ],
    "statement": "Don't make users care about uninteresting implementation details: unify related concepts (sequences, numbers, statements) into a single general mechanism that behaves consistently.",
    "time_local": "2009-02-03 10:53:00",
    "who": "Guido van Rossum"
   },
   {
    "id": "C7",
    "quote": "A truncating division operator (//) was added to the language to provide the same functionality. In addition, a mechanism (\\\"from __future__ import division\\\") was introduced by which the new integer division semantics could be enabled. ... the correct behavior has become the default behavior in Py…",
    "quote_row": "written:2009-03-10_the-problem-with-integer-division:fixing-integer-division",
    "rows": [
     "written:2009-03-10_the-problem-with-integer-division:fixing-integer-division",
     "written:2009-03-31_the-great-or-grand-renaming:two-phase-transition-with-macros",
     "written:2010-06-21_new-style-classes:new-style-as-feature-not-replacement",
     "written:2013-11-10_the-story-of-none-true-and-false-and-an-:adding-a-builtin-must-not-break-existing-code",
     "written:2013-11-10_the-story-of-none-true-and-false-and-an-:new-builtins-are-cheap-to-add",
     "written:2013-11-10_the-story-of-none-true-and-false-and-an-:new-keywords-kill-or-reshape-features",
     "written:2013-11-10_the-story-of-none-true-and-false-and-an-:reserved-words-impose-a-global-cost"
    ],
    "statement": "Preserve backwards compatibility and evolve the language gradually, introducing changes through opt-ins (from __future__), deprecation campaigns, and reluctantly added keywords.",
